114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E ( The successful path begins when the user enters a small supported portfolio with positive monetary values and valid currencies. The application validates and normalizes the identifiers, retrieves market and exchange-rate data, aligns the observations, and presents the current allocation and portfolio result. The user can then inspect return, risk, and foreign-exchange contribution, compare the portfolio with the selected benchmark, and optionally examine an optimized reference allocation or a simulation. If authenticated, the user may record a decision and rationale in a private journal.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E ( Alternative paths address conditions that do not necessarily invalidate the entire analysis. A direct index series may be unavailable while a clearly labelled ETF proxy remains acceptable; an international market may have a different latest session; or a cached snapshot may still satisfy the selected historical range. The requirement in each case is transparency. The interface must identify the source, effective period, and fallback, and it must withhold any relative metric that cannot be calculated on compatible observations.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 59 0 R
/E ( Error paths prevent invalid state from reaching the numerical engine. Unsupported symbols, non-positive position values, duplicate instruments, unknown currency codes, insufficient observations, and infeasible caps produce actionable messages. When the user changes an input after an analysis has been generated, the old result must be cleared or marked invalid. This rule prevents a mismatch between the visible portfolio and the figures retained from a previous request.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
