122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 60 0 R
/T (2.2.ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA FOR FINANCIAL AND ALLOCATION IDENTITIES)
/E (2.2.ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA FOR FINANCIAL AND ALLOCATION IDENTITIES)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E ( Financial methodology was converted into observable pass or fail conditions. Holding-level or grouped return contributions must reconcile with portfolio return. Euler component risks must reconcile with annualized portfolio volatility. Foreign-exchange components must reconcile with the VND-denominated holding return. These conditions are more useful than checking a hard-coded output because market data can change while the identity remains valid.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E ( Optimization acceptance combines mathematical and interface conditions. Proposed weights must sum to 100%, remain non-negative, obey per-instrument and commodity-group caps, and use the same observations as the current allocation. The application must detect infeasibility before showing a proposal. Minimum-volatility and approximate maximum-Sharpe objectives are tested separately, and convergence status must be displayed rather than inferred from the existence of a result.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 60 0 R
/E ( Simulation acceptance focuses on reproducibility and interpretation. A fixed seed must generate identical percentile bands and tail statistics. Flat historical returns must produce a flat result, and short or invalid samples must be rejected. The interface must state that the percentile fan and loss proportion are outputs of the bootstrap model rather than validated probabilities of future market events.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
